cedures you've got these methods that  run on the database itself stored  procedures have certain requirements  sometimes like maybe they require a  super user if you're running on a  managed database we don't give you a  proper super user because we have this  um we have to protect the database for  you we have an sla a service level  agreement where we guarantee that the  database is going to be up  all the time for you right like you you  hear some people talk about the nines  the nines is your sort of percent of um  how much uptime we guarantee cloud sql  for example our managed offering is  99.95  available three and a half nines which  means you know it's like 20 minutes of  downtime a month or something like that  so  with these extensions  or the the stored procedures we have to  guard that up time which means we don't  give you everything so if you've got a  system that requires that  a managed system might not work for you  okay that actually makes a lot of sense  and i appreciate you for bringing in  that perspective  so now my question is what about someone  that uh  maybe doesn't have uh their database in  the cloud yet but they're thinking about  switching over what's the process for  that right so you know you're talking  about like we call it the green field  where um new application uh a new  company that's thinking like where  should i put my stuff everything's  moving to the cloud like let's be to be  clear that this what we're seeing in the  industry right is everyone wants their  data in the cloud for a whole host of  reasons you want to run analytics on  your data um you've got a global  situation where you've got an  application that needs global access  you're going to be in the cloud so i  always say if you haven't got something  already  start with the managed service right you  don't want to set up all the boilerplate  stuff that you're going to have to do to  run your own database the hardware  management all the operating system and  security updates managing the database  health itself doing all you know making  sure that the machine is healthy with a  managed service all that's handled for  you you don't have to do any of that  stuff so if you're starting fresh  absolutely start with a managed service  well you just said it right there you're  kind of um i love it when you're  starting a new project and you get to  like set everything up from scratch but  a lot of us are dealing with a lot of  software that already exists like what  do you do in that scenario so if you've  got one of those legacy systems like i  said right like the 20-year application  you're working with there's a few  different ways you could do it there's  if your systems are all really tightly  integrated and you've got it running in  like a tight cluster  of machines or something you can just do  that the the lift and shift right just  take the entire thing throw it up in a  vm and now instead of running in your  closet you're running in our closet but  we handle all the machine health for you  so that's absolutely a valid strategy if  you need to get out of where you're at  and get to the cloud first just put  everything in a vm and get going you do  want to split the data out though right  so strategically speaking if you if your  requirements get to the cloud first do  that lift and shift up then start  working on splitting off your data  there are cases though where that might  not be the case if you've got an  application that  for  compliance reasons or other you have to  have the application local but your data  has now grown beyond where it can handle  and you need to just get the data out  splitting the data out is totally fine  too  but there's a there's some problems  there right so the internet the  internet's a problem right  so  if you are in a situation where you want  to keep your application but you need to  move the data that's fine but you should  be aware that you'll have to handle that  on the application side so  great connection to your data center  maybe you've got a 30 millisecond ping  time round trip right  if you're running locally you're ping to  your databases less than a millisecond  for a really well-tuned relational  database so all of a sudden your times  30 but it's not even that kind because  let's say for example you're only  allowing ssl connections to your  database the tls handshake to establish  that connection takes two round trips  back and forth to establish the  handshake before you get to the data so  now it's not 30 milliseconds to get your  answer it's 100 milliseconds to get your  answer and then a bunch of apps like  make a bunch of queries in a row and so  that just like every one of those  extends it out so  what  what should people do in that kind of  like what do you do about that i mean  you can't fix the internet the internet  you're kind of stuck with but the like  the tons of  connections the queries that you talked  about right some applications because  they're used to this quick turnaround  time they'll just spin up a connection  ask their questions shut down the  connection if you do that a couple  hundred times and your round trip is now  not so quick that's terr that's gonna  like wreck your your uh responsive ui so  instead  the first thing that i tell people is  the first thing you want to do is what  we call client-side connection pooling  most libraries that you connect with so  there's like for python we recommend  sql alchemy is a great library to use  for connecting to your relational  databases there's objects that you use  to create the connection and there's a  connection object and there's a pool  object that pool is a connection pool  that will establish you a connection to  the database and it holds it open for  whatever you specify that means if  you're making these like hundreds of  queries you know every second that's  great it'll establish one connection run  all your queries until there's some idle  time 