nch of like  little knobs to make that easier we've  got a service it's called database  migration service dms  and that basically handles it still uses  the database native ways to do it so  like for example on postgres we use pg  logical as a the way we do migrations  and it will  handle all the connections all the  connectivity you define  a source object your source database in  the ui and then it creates a cloud sql  instance for you and then it moves it  now keeping in mind this is only for  managed service so if you're moving to a  managed service this is the tool to do  it if you want to run your own in a vm  you're kind of on your own because it's  not managed for you so you have to do  all of these pieces you can still do it  there's still ways to set up external  replicas uh and external primary  instances to do it  but you're on your own so dms is kind of  that easy way to move to a managed  service okay that's super cool because i  have definitely lived the um try to  schedule a window where you can shut  everything down with all the people  involved and then hope everything works  right and you don't have to like revert  or whatever thing and that  that's hard so it's nice to know that  there's some tools to make that easier  now so all right to summarize what we  just learned today just uh just to recap  everything you came into it  i still want to hold up that my managed  databases can and should be used in a  lot of instances but there are other  instances where it doesn't make sense to  use them where there's some capability  that you need that's not provided yet um  or maybe just the way your system is set  up uh but if you're trying to transfer  over then there's database management  system which makes it a lot smoother of  a process did i did i did i get that  exactly and it all comes down to your  requirements right like you said nothing  wrong with running a database in the vm  as long as you're comfortable managing  it if you don't want to wo